Ladybird’s MatchPattern parses the scheme/host/path grammar once and
matches a URL’s path together with its query string, ignoring the
port and the fragment. WebKit’s WebExtensionMatchPattern does the
same, with matchesURL() and a matchesPattern() that compares two
patterns, and its allHostsAndSchemesMatchPattern() is the “<all_urls>”
case; at injection time WebCore’s UserContentURLPattern::
matchesPatterns() checks the allowlist and blocklist. Blink’s
URLPattern (extensions/common/url_pattern.cc) splits a pattern into
scheme, host, port, and path with MatchesURL(), and UserScript::
MatchesURL() applies exclude patterns and globs first, then the
include patterns. Gecko’s MatchPattern (MatchPattern.cpp) keeps
mScheme, mDomain, and mPath, and MatchPatternSet::Matches() ORs a
manifest’s patterns together; its MozDocumentMatcher adds exclusion
and glob handling on top. Ladybird folds the query string into the
path match, as Blink and Gecko do; WebKit does too, through
UserContentURLPattern.

A match pattern’s host goes through the URL host parser, so Ladybird
rejects a malformed host and keeps every other one in canonical form.
Blink’s URLPattern::Parse() does the same: It rejects any host that
net::CanonicalizeHost() fails on. WebKit’s WebExtensionMatchPattern,
through UserContentURLPattern, rejects credentials, ports, and
malformed IPv6 brackets, but doesn’t canonicalize. And Gecko’s
MatchPatternCore copies the host as written (dropping IPv6 brackets),
so it accepts more host text than Ladybird does.
